From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Largo <andi@idogawa.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0100
Subject: MathUtils.ToString(BigInteger, radix) in subquadratic time

It divided the whole number by the radix once per digit: quadratic, with a
BigInteger division per digit, so a million-bit number took ~108 s. A
power-of-two radix now reads the bits; any other radix splits the number by
powers of the radix (divide and conquer) down to pieces of 8 words, which are
divided in place by the largest power of the radix that fits in a uint. On
.NET 5 to 8, whose BigInteger.DivRem is quadratic, the large splits use
Barrett reduction with a Newton reciprocal (multiplications only). The output
is the same: upper case, '-' for a negative number.

---
